# bootaa64.efi + openabl provenance

Vendored ROCKNIX GRUB binary, extracted verbatim from the FAT partition
(EFI/BOOT/bootaa64.efi) of the official release image:

  release:  https://github.com/ROCKNIX/distribution/releases/tag/20260701
  asset:    ROCKNIX-SM8250.aarch64-20260701.img.gz
  img.gz sha256: c4bd6943abe46b438b97d66060b02b8b4a9169c99972b3c8cbcfba94a8228df0
  bootaa64.efi sha256: d6d0939dce9462f8ad675e4f26d833f3833e0e0a2461a2d4e7b5e25247801675

It is GRUB 2.14-rc1 built by ROCKNIX (GPL-3.0-or-later; source: ROCKNIX
distribution repo, packages/tools/grub: package.mk + 5 patches, notably
grub-silent-boot, framebuffer rotation, and the partfind/strsearch rawsearch
commands their board-autodetect grub.cfg uses). Vendored rather than
self-built so the SM8250 boot chain uses the exact binary ROCKNIX ships and
tests against the same ABL. To self-build instead: ALARM's grub +
grub-mkimage -O arm64-efi -p /boot/grub with ROCKNIX's module list (their
package.mk makeinstall_target), plus their patches if the autodetect cfg
commands are wanted.

Note: the RP5's and Flip 2's factory ABL already provides the UEFI environment
that chainloads this bootaa64.efi. "tests against the same ABL" above refers to
ROCKNIX validating this GRUB binary against that factory ABL. GRUB stays only
until OpenABL is required.

grubenv is the pristine environment block from the same image. grubenv on
/flash is user state (saved menu choice) - never overwritten on upgrade.

grub.cfg.rocknix-reference is the GENERATED grub.cfg from the same image
(boot=LABEL LibreELEC-style cmdline, savedefault menu, one entry per board) -
kept as a reference only. Our shipped grub.cfg is the pocknix-authored
static one: root=PARTUUID (re-pinned by pocknix-update-grub), one entry per
board and panel revision, timeout 0, and no `search`: every entry boots from
the FAT GRUB was loaded from, which holds this install's kernel.


# openabl kit (SM8250)

abl_signed-SM8250.elf is NOT committed here - the PKGBUILD fetches it from the
same pinned OpenABL release the sm8550 package uses:

  release:  https://github.com/OpenHandheldCollective/OpenABL/releases/tag/v1.2
  asset:    openabl-v1.2.tar.gz (one folder per chipset)
  source:   openabl-v1.2-source.tar.xz on the same release (the GPL-3.0 corresponding source)
  tarball sha512: 41f8b0460bc5c2cbbec312911377e52c41eda7c4dcd365bc76486d81cf4da0e9
                  072df00edaf3afacb87565d856487d0aac3c8b5506d46b9ea6c901c319635492
  SM8250/abl_signed-SM8250.elf sha256 (release's own sidecar, re-checked in prepare()):
                  628dbff74f5b570b897c14725f2c36ff2ca5fe018998e2c248a3d15d906ccef9

OpenABL is not the ROCKNIX ABL v1.2 the kit shipped before (different bytes,
same version label). It boots Boot Loader Spec entries (loader/entries/*.conf)
ahead of EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI, so a FAT carrying both GRUB and the entry
written by pocknix-bls-deploy boots GRUB on a factory ABL and the entry here.

The four kit scripts are verbatim copies of ROCKNIX's
packages/tools/openabl/sources/ (distribution next 13d241a7d3), with %DEVICE%
-> SM8250 (their package.mk substitution). Keep the two packages' copies
identical; diff both against the ROCKNIX tree when advancing the sync pin.

Nothing flashes it automatically. The in-OS path is pocknix-update-abl; the
kit's Android scripts remain the path for a device that cannot boot pocknix.
